# ui-protect-on.caddy -- imported INSIDE the catch-all `handle` of the n8n
# site (editor UI, /rest API, test webhooks, push) when UI_PROTECT=on.
# Production webhooks, forms, MCP and the health paths are matched by earlier
# handles and never pass through this file.
#
# Two layers, evaluated in THIS order:
#   1. IP allow-list: a client outside UI_ALLOW_CIDR gets a bare 403 and is
#      never offered a password prompt.
#   2. HTTP basic auth for everybody else (401 challenge, then n8n's own login).
#
# The `route` wrapper is ESSENTIAL (verified): inside a plain handle block
# Caddy re-orders directives by its built-in directive order, which places
# basic_auth BEFORE respond -- clients outside the CIDR would then get a 401
# challenge and only see the 403 after typing a correct password. `route`
# keeps the written order.
#
# UI_ALLOW_CIDR: space-separated CIDRs, e.g. "203.0.113.0/24 2001:db8::/32".
#   UNSET -> the default "0.0.0.0/0 ::/0" allows every address through to
#   basic auth. The default applies only when the variable is unset; a value
#   that is set but EMPTY leaves `remote_ip` without ranges and `not` then
#   matches everyone -> 403 for all (compose must not pass an empty value).
#   `remote_ip` is the TCP peer address: a request made on the Docker host
#   itself arrives from the bridge gateway (172.x.0.1), not 127.0.0.1, and a
#   proxy in front of Caddy would need `trusted_proxies` + the client_ip
#   matcher instead.
# UI_BASIC_AUTH_USER / UI_BASIC_AUTH_HASH: the hash is the output of
#   `docker compose exec caddy caddy hash-password --plaintext '...'` (bcrypt,
#   `$2a$14$...`). In .env it MUST be single-quoted ('$2a$14$...') or every
#   `$` doubled, otherwise Compose interpolation silently eats the `$...`
#   segments and no password ever matches (verified). Both values must be
#   non-empty or validation fails ("username and password cannot be empty").
#
# Multi-line rule: `basic_auth { user hash }` on one line fails to parse.
route {
	@denied not remote_ip {$UI_ALLOW_CIDR:0.0.0.0/0 ::/0}
	respond @denied 403
	basic_auth {
		{$UI_BASIC_AUTH_USER} {$UI_BASIC_AUTH_HASH}
	}
}
